WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.920
- Welcome to this round table discussion,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.780
on the 'Teaching Archive."

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.080
I'm Alice Kaplan.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.430
I'm the director of the
Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.890
And I will be moderating today.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:22.553
Allow me to briefly introduce
our authors and participants.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.620
First, Rachel Sagner Buurma,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.890
teaches English literature
at Swarthmore College,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:32.350
where she co-directs the
Aydelotte Foundation,

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:33.370
a center supporting

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.500
interdisciplinary liberal arts education.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.580
Her work focuses on 18th and 19th century

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.650
literature and print culture,

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.260
the history of the novel,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.300
20th century Anglo-American
literary criticism

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.950
and the history of higher education.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.320
She's working on a book on
the social literary critical

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.580
origins of narrative omniscience,

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.910
and she has another book project

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:01.050
on how Victoria novelists
did their research.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.730
Laura Heffernan teaches English literature

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.860
at the University of North Florida.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.720
And her focus is 19th and
20th century literature

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.140
in culture, histories of reading

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.780
and histories of literary criticism.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.500
She teaches courses on
the history of the novel,

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.910
modernist literature,

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.090
digital methods in literary study

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.970
and later periods of British literature.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.060
She's currently serving as director

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.110
of UNF's Digital Humanities Institute.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.260
Together, they have published
the "Teaching Archive,"

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.180
a groundbreaking approach
to literary history,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.520
that takes us inside the classrooms

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.410
of major literary critics,

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:43.430
through their class notes and archives.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.440
And what better time than now,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.870
after this year of exile
from our own classrooms

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:53.210
to acknowledge the classroom
experience as a prime incubator

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.170
of critical thought.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:02:00.090
Eye opening, paradigm shifting,
restorative, humbling,

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.280
all these words came to mind

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.720
as I was reading the "Teaching Archive."

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.520
We are, note that we are
putting a link to the book

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.800
with the University of Chicago Press

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.843
discount on the chat feature.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.660
Rachel Sagner Buurma

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.710
and Laura Heffernan will be
joined today by Caleb Smith

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:21.010
professor of English and American studies

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:22.830
at Yale university,

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.170
who also teaches American literature

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.710
at MacDougall-Walker
Correctional Institution

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:32.140
through the Yale Prison
Education Initiative.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.950
Caleb Smith's work
explores the contradictions

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.170
in American history between
Liberty and enslavement,

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:42.640
democracy and domination,
secularism and fanaticism.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.477
And you can read a chapter
from his forthcoming book,

00:02:45.477 --> 00:02:49.280
"Disciplines of Attention
in a Secular Age"

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.500
in critical inquiry.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.650
Now I'd like to turn the
floor to Rachel and Laura,

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.800
who will give an overview
of "The Teaching Archive."

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.590
Caleb Smith will respond,

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:02.680
and then we will open
the floor to questions.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.450
So please post your questions at any time

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:08.380
during the presentations in the Q and A,

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.620
and don't hesitate to use the chat

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.080
for your expressions of
interest and enthusiasm,

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.403
and for relevant bibliography.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.440
Rachel and Laura.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:24.810
- Alice, thank you so much
for that lovely introduction.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.083
Can you see me okay?

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:28.110
Great.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.570
And thanks to Diane and Sarah

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.350
and the rest of the Whitney
humanities stuff also.

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.240
Thanks also to Caleb for joining us today,

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:36.780
we're really looking forward

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.383
to our conversation with you.

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.060
So "The Teaching Archive"
recovers the everyday teaching

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.180
of literary scholars,

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.480
over the course of the 20th century,

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:47.780
in order to write a new history

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.670
for the discipline of English studies.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.210
And this book really began
with our dissatisfaction

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:55.890
with existing approaches to
disciplinary history so far

00:03:56.913 --> 00:03:58.650
and with the ways in which
disciplinary practice

00:03:58.650 --> 00:03:59.530
get represented

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:02.233
and contemporary battles
over reading methods.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.630
Existing disciplinary histories

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:05.970
tend to take a selective

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:09.100
and we think kind of distant
view of our profession.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.600
We're thinking here of Gerald
Grass, professing literature,

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.603
or John Guillory's Cultural Capital,

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.440
to the extent that these
disciplinary history

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.140
has performed research on
art discipline history,

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.270
their research tends to
survey the disciplines

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:23.470
most self-conscious moments,

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.970
rather than it's kind of
practical everyday ones.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:29.290
Traditional disciplinary history

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:30.164
and English studies tends to turn

00:04:30.164 --> 00:04:32.230
to methodological manifestos,

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:34.310
and professional addresses,

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:36.623
mission statements and public feuds.

00:04:38.346 --> 00:04:40.310
And it tends to produce
a sense of our past

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.780
that emphasizes innovation
and originality in a rupture,

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:46.470
rather than the everyday continuities,

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:50.373
that we think are more
familiar to everyday practice.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.740
So for us, and we think for many others,

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.320
this produces a kind of alienating

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.113
and not so useful account
of our profession.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:06.850
This method Wars are not
a good representation

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:08.397
of the everyday practices
of the discipline,

00:05:08.397 --> 00:05:09.870
and the day-to-day work of it.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:13.100
The longer continuities that
extended cost of our training,

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.713
and lead us together.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.380
Disciplinary history is also,

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.010
we noticed tended to
ignore a really part of our

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.720
a really major part of
our professional lives,

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.640
of our classroom teaching.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:24.850
There's been a tendency,

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.990
just a sort of default tendency to assume,

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:28.650
that English professors practice some form

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:30.040
of close reading in the classroom

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.650
no matter what they were
actually doing in their research.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.050
And there's recurring
reference to just a small set

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.780
of pedagogical theoretical texts,

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.310
like Practical Criticism
or The Well Wrought Urn,

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:42.530
and textbooks,

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:44.620
like "Understanding
Poetry" books of Warren,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:49.620
tend to stand in for the
real history of teaching.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.870
So we decided what we wanted
to do is take a closer look

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.230
at what and how professors have taught,

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.240
by seeking out their teaching papers.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.230
And so what we did do that
was come here to Yale first,

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:01.940
to The Beinecke ,

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.880
to look at Cleanth
Brooks' teaching papers.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.310
We are curious to know whether

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.190
and how Brooks' classroom teaching

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.570
matched his account in
The Well Wrought Urn,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.670
of how and why an English
professor should cultivate

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:14.360
students' appreciation for aesthetic form.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.390
And the really unique thing
that we never found again

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:20.130
that we found in Brooks'
papers was a transcription

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:23.293
of his modern poetry class
from the early 1960s.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:25.990
He had contracted with,

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:27.070
I think random house

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:29.570
to write a book based on those lectures.

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.490
And so he had employed a stenographer,

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.560
to transcribe every single thing,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.600
the back and forth between
himself and the students,

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.400
even like the initial
chat at the beginning

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.333
of the class period.

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:44.713
And we never got to see that again.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:47.920
After working on Brooks though,

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.040
we decided that we wanted to move outwards

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.550
and look at other kinds of institutions.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:58.550
We were really curious to
know what teaching looks like

00:06:58.648 --> 00:07:02.610
at the kinds of institutions
in which most people

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.490
who to go to college,
who go to college right?

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:05.830
I.e not Yale,

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:08.580
even though most disciplinary
histories tend to center

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:10.960
on elite institutions like Yale.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.030
And Laura is gonna tell you
a little bit about the kinds

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.860
of places, the kinds of
different kinds of institutions,

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:18.823
where we went to look at classrooms.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.880
- Yes, so we (audio drives
away the sound of the speaker).

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:29.410
So after we looked at
Brooks's papers at Yale,

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:32.690
we went to go look at J. Saunders Redding,

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:34.930
J. Saunders Redding archive,

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:38.090
his teaching papers
from Hampton University.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:41.150
We looked at Caroline
Spurgeon's teaching papers

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:42.910
at Bedford Women's College

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.490
from the early 20th century.

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:47.370
We looked at T.S Eliot's papers

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.970
from the Worker's Educational
Association tutorial.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:52.740
He taught, which we'll
talk about in a minute.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.610
We looked at Alan Richardson's
teaching papers at Cambridge

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.320
and Edith Rickert's teaching papers

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:58.940
at University of Chicago.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:01.740
We also looked at Simon J. Ortiz

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.170
and what he had taught at Marin College

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:05.810
in Northern California

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:10.020
and the University of New
Mexico and DNA college.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:13.840
And we looked at Josephine
Miles, her teaching at Berkeley

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.970
we looked at Josefina
Niggli teaching drama

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:20.573
at Western Carolina college in the 1950s.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.210
We actually looked at many more archives

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:24.830
then we ended up, including in the book.

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:27.040
And as we'll describe today

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.520
each of the places that we went

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.750
sort of required us to
develop a new approach

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:34.910
to our research,

00:08:34.910 --> 00:08:38.830
and each place really taught
us something different.

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:41.970
So we'll start talking today

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.340
the first example we
will take you through is

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:47.830
T.S Eliot's modern English
literature tutorial,

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:51.810
which he taught here at
Sutton Grammar School

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:56.810
just outside of London
from 1916 through 1919.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:59.040
So during world war one.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.670
Eliot taught a couple of
extension courses in these years,

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:07.670
but he, the this course
was a course taught

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:09.130
through the University of London

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:10.500
but in partnership with

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:12.930
the Worker's Educational Association.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:16.830
And so it was a three-year
tutorial course.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.450
And one of the things we had to do

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:22.830
was go look at a bunch of
different WEA tutorial syllabi,

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:27.350
to kind of understand
the institutional values

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:30.500
of these particular extension courses.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:34.720
So WEA syllabi reflected the fact

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.240
that these tutorials were meant to be

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:39.970
an exercising kind of deep equality.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:42.120
Teachers were fellow students,

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.400
students were fellow teachers,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:49.400
and they agreed to study
together for three years.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:52.270
It was essentially a
community building exercise.

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:56.040
So communities decided what
courses they wanted to take,

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:57.810
and then once they had
embarked on a course

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.390
with a tutor of their choosing,

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:04.000
they would work together
to revise the course list

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.053
in years two and three of the syllabus.

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:10.900
By and large, these
students kind of rejected

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:12.850
the university's schemes

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:15.760
to kind of elevate the best among them,

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:18.240
to a place in the university.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:22.127
For them the point was really
to extend study outside

00:10:22.127 --> 00:10:24.290
of the seminar itself.

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:27.240
So WEA students often retaught

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.410
their classes to other community members.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:32.420
They have held study groups,
including family members.

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:36.170
And the idea was that study
would create more study

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:39.610
rather than individual
achievement or accreditation.

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:42.570
And so when we looked at Eliot's syllabi,

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:44.080
it was really clear to us

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:49.060
that he learned how to adjust
his pedagogy to the WEA.

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:50.690
He made significant changes

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:52.780
between his first and his second year.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:55.030
And then his second and his third year,

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.860
he responded to what the
students wanted to learn.

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:00.580
And he slowed down (chuckles).

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:01.930
This is probably familiar to those

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:03.130
of you who here are teachers,

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:04.700
he became much less ambitious

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:06.590
in what he thought he could cover,

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:08.020
in the second and third years.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:09.360
And most notably,

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:11.870
he moved from a syllabus
that was organized

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:13.873
around the individual author,

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:17.670
to a syllabus that was
deeply interconnected,

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:19.010
and that brought back to life

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:22.740
the material working world
of Elizabethan writers.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:25.120
So this had a practical purpose for him,

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:27.550
it helps students engage
in individual study

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:29.740
to follow up their particular interests

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:31.300
and to write seminar papers

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.600
that would then continue to be useful

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.873
and to connect back up with
class discussion in later weeks.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:41.190
But it also sort of allowed the class to

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:46.180
reimagine these writers as
workers, as working writers.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:48.440
You can see in the Elizabethan
Literature syllabus

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.060
I have up here,

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.300
he's teaching not an author based syllabus

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:54.680
but he's teaching popular
festival and religious rite.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:57.530
In week one Renaissance Humanism,

00:11:57.530 --> 00:11:58.860
the Elizabethan Stage,

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:00.780
students are learning
a lot about the context

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:02.893
in which these writers were working.

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:08.550
- And once you see how
Eliot adapted his teaching

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.350
to the values of the WEA,

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:11.860
to the workers education association

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.150
you can also see really dramatically,

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:17.120
how his classroom teaching
shaped his public writing.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:18.550
So while Eliot was teaching his

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:19.940
Elizabethan Literature seminar,

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:21.867
he was also writing his
famous book of essays,

00:12:21.867 --> 00:12:23.500
"The Sacred Wood,"

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:25.540
which if you don't know it as
a book really often credited

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:26.950
with changing literary tastes

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:28.800
in the Anglophone world of letters.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.570
And it's something more spare and formal

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:32.330
and elites saying goodbye to Tennyson

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:34.637
and hello to Dryden and done.

00:12:34.637 --> 00:12:36.470
"The Sacred Wood" includes essays

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:39.640
on figures like Marlow
and Massingir and Johnson,

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:42.903
whose plays Eliot had read
with his students in a seminar.

00:12:43.966 --> 00:12:46.110
But the connections are
even much deeper than that.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:48.100
Because what you really see in the idea

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:49.470
of literary tradition

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:52.110
that Eliot famously talks
about in "The Sacred Wood,"

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:56.090
is how the WEA impacted
Eliot sense of that tradition

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:57.620
and of literary value.

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:00.830
You really see in "The Sacred
Wood" a sense of writing

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:04.180
as important, not for its
singularity and originality,

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:05.900
but for the extent to which it makes

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:07.710
further writing possible.

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:09.710
So while most readings
of "The sacred Wood"

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:12.170
focus on literary tradition as elite

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:14.110
and Eliot's view of literary
tradition as an elite

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:14.943
and specialist,

00:13:14.943 --> 00:13:18.620
once you see Eliot's
classroom, you really realize

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:20.990
that that view of literary
tradition is much more

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.360
about workaday writing
and the possibilities

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:24.750
of collective writing.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:26.790
And then this was an
insight that we began to

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:28.370
see everywhere we looked,

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:30.760
after we realized this
about Eliot's classroom

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:34.920
sort of being inside and coded
inside "The Sacred Wood."

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:36.750
We realized that classrooms live inside

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:38.850
and have strongly shaped many, many

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:42.040
of the major works of criticism
that we read and teach.

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:44.180
So we realized, for example,
that Edmund Wilson's

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:46.700
historical interpretation
of literature essay

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:49.610
grew out of his varieties in
19th century criticism course

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:52.560
that he taught at Chicago in 1939.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.470
We came to see that
Caroline Spurgeon's still

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:58.040
cited work "Shakespeare's
Imagery And What It Tells Us,"

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:00.000
that she published in the 1930s,

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.253
grew out of the art of reading course

00:14:01.253 --> 00:14:03.850
that she taught at
Bedford College for Women

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:06.010
in the 19 teens.

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:08.720
And we saw how Simon Ortiz
had worked out the ideas

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:10.920
for his important essay
towards the National Indian

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:11.790
literature,

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:14.380
while he was revising his
native American Lit course

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:19.143
between 1976 and 1979 for
students at Marin college.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:24.180
So we're gonna give another example

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:25.890
of how this happens.

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:30.080
- So the second example,
this is the course book list,

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:33.690
for Josephine Miles' English 1A course.

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:35.800
Miles was a poet and a professor.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.680
She was the first woman tenured in

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.680
the English department at Berkeley.

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:42.140
And she taught a bunch
of different courses

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:42.973
in the department.

00:14:42.973 --> 00:14:46.230
She taught a very popular
verse composition course,

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:47.700
she taught upper level seminars

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:50.330
and graduate seminars on the lyric,

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:54.040
and on 17th century poetry
and research methods.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.570
But she most commonly taught English 1A

00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:58.460
which was essentially the
first year writing course

00:14:58.460 --> 00:14:59.293
at Berkeley.

00:14:59.293 --> 00:15:02.190
And she taught it for over three decades.

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:05.400
You can see here, well
you maybe can't quite see

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:07.100
but the book list that she's jotted down

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:09.580
for her students includes a style guide,

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:13.280
a dictionary, also Virginia
Woolf's Mrs Dalloway,

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:15.540
a modern classic of their choosing

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:16.610
so they can choose Mann

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:19.580
or Proust or Joyce or Shaw, or O'Neil

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:23.060
a modern bestseller,
she says see a scrap lists,

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:25.060
Story Magazine, Poetry Magazine

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:27.470
Harper's, The Southern
Review, The New Yorker,

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:31.310
The New Republic and A Specialty Magazine.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:33.810
Miles has every archive we want to

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:36.150
people keep their teaching
notes very differently,

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:39.970
Miles kept tons of teaching
notes on these tiny

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:42.780
pieces of notebook paper in tiny script.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:46.180
And she did keep notes for
every semester of English 1A

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:47.013
that she taught,

00:15:47.013 --> 00:15:49.190
and the notes really
testify to the fact that

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:52.970
it was a very workshop style course.

00:15:52.970 --> 00:15:54.470
She said, in fact to the students

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:57.240
at the beginning of the fall, 1940 course,

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:00.270
she described it as a lab
or machine tool course

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:03.513
where the prof doesn't tell
and just watch his practice.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:06.850
And she would often begin this course

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:08.550
with what she called the corner essay.

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:10.930
She would send students
out to a street corner,

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:13.890
usually the corner of Oxford
University in Berkeley.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:15.980
And she'd asked them to
stand there for an hour,

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:18.600
and then write an essay about it.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.460
And quite often the
students would give her

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:22.030
a first draft back,

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:24.010
that was simply a chronological report

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:24.860
of everything they'd seen

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:26.570
while they were standing
on the street corner.

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:29.260
And she would work with
them to revise the essays,

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:31.890
so that the essays would have perspective.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:34.760
So that they would order a little bit more

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.700
the sense data that they had acquired,

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:38.950
give a little bit more of
a narrative or a story,

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:40.930
give meaning to what they had seen.

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:43.300
And that kind of work
of taking perspective

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:44.830
was something that Miles thought about

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:47.770
as beginning at the level of the sentence.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:49.860
So the writer of a sentence

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:54.680
orders the elements in the
sentence through syntax,

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:56.230
through subordinating clauses,

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:58.730
connecting and prepositions.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:01.210
And so Miles had students work

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:04.410
on sentences over and over in English 1A.

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:07.610
She had them write 50
words sentences together,

00:17:07.610 --> 00:17:09.680
she had them choose a
sentence from a reading

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:12.230
and analyze its function
and its transition,

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:13.680
she had them write a sentence

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:17.660
that encapsulated a belief that they held.

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:18.870
And she you know

00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:21.633
they worked every single day,
essentially on sentences.

00:17:23.090 --> 00:17:25.420
- And this interest in language
at the level of the sentence

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:28.750
went alongside the quantitative
work Miles was doing

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:30.810
from the 1940s forward.

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:32.957
She was working with a big
Corpus of English poetry

00:17:32.957 --> 00:17:35.620
to count the vocabulary of each era.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.960
Trying to characterize the vocabulary

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:42.290
that poets had to work with as
sort of to hand it each era,

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:44.290
as phrasal or clausal is focused

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:46.990
either on adjectives basically or nouns.

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:48.860
If we could have table a from continuity

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:53.600
you can see some of her
results there up to the left.

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:55.590
So we realize while doing this work also

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:58.080
that Miles is one of
the foundational figures

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.420
and distant reading
and digital humanities.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:02.620
In addition to the hand counted data

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:04.370
that dozens of monographs she published,

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:06.790
which she helped her graduate
students help her with

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:08.330
quite a lot.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:11.450
In the 1940s, she also created
a computerized concordance

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:13.470
to the complete works of John Dryden.

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:17.460
She inherited this concordance
to John Dryden project

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.110
from a senior colleague

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:20.627
and ended up collaborating
with her graduate students

00:18:20.627 --> 00:18:23.255
and with the women and the
Electrical Engineering department

00:18:23.255 --> 00:18:25.883
at Berkeley, that ran the IBM machines.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:31.690
So Roberto Busa, father
Busa is off really still

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:35.650
to this day known as the
"Father of Digital Humanities"

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:38.840
and a computationally
assisted literary study.

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:41.140
But Miles made her concordance of Dryden

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:43.780
far before he made his and really,

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:46.550
she is really we think one of the founders

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:49.650
of distant reading in
a way that Busa isn't.

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:51.864
And that's a kind of dramatic
like example of how looking

00:18:51.864 --> 00:18:54.590
at teaching archives can
totally change the way we think

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:56.720
about the history of art discipline.

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:57.553
But in general,

00:18:57.553 --> 00:18:59.840
looking at teaching archives
gives us a really new view

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:01.080
of a few different things,

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:03.130
which we're just going to
chat about very briefly

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:04.960
before we turn to our
conversation with Caleb

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:06.253
and with the rest of you.

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:10.040
Across all the archives in the
classroom that we looked at,

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:13.340
we see a really different
history of method broadly.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:15.490
We see not a kind of contrapuntal

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:19.000
set of arguments played
out in publications,

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:20.910
but we see especially a longer

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.340
and more nuanced kind of historicism

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:24.800
is just an example.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:26.400
But we also find a really different view

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:28.030
of who has made art discipline.

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:30.380
Because once you realize
that teachers have made

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:33.390
teachers of literature
have made art discipline,

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:35.430
you have a lot more
possibilities (chuckles)

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:39.493
for thinking about it's
fashioning and it's shaping.

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:44.880
Looking at The Teaching Archives
also has given us a really

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:48.030
different view of what has
been taught in the classroom.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:51.300
So yes, canonical texts
and all of these archives,

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:54.190
but also a long, long tail
of less canonical texts,

00:19:54.190 --> 00:19:56.890
and in fact, totally unliterary texts.

00:19:56.890 --> 00:19:59.170
Maybe as much as half of
what's taught in classrooms

00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:00.820
we think is not canonical at all.

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:04.660
So you still get Shakespeare,
you still get Mrs Dalloway,

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:07.880
but you also have all these other things.

00:20:07.880 --> 00:20:11.310
And then the last thing
we wanted to touch on

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:13.670
the other thing that we saw
throughout these archives

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:15.740
is how closely teaching and research

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:18.360
and publication are intertwined,

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:19.790
and the way that we think they still are

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:21.570
for most practitioners today.

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:23.470
Despite the fact that the
modern research university

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:26.710
is really done its very
best to pull them apart.

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:28.550
So the very idea that we can have

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:30.230
teaching faculty or adjuncts,

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:31.580
like people who are academic workers,

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:34.950
who are not imagined as
doing research at all

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:36.440
as part of their job,

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:39.400
is a huge part of this obviously.

00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:42.450
As our tenure process is and
all kinds of other mechanisms

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:44.410
but we think it's a real
testament to the strength

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:46.330
of the actual long history,

00:20:46.330 --> 00:20:48.730
of teaching and research being intertwined

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:52.410
that so many of us continue to
experience them as connected.

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:54.620
Despite all these
administrative mechanisms

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:56.200
that make us pull them apart.

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:58.552
Laura, is there anything
else you want to say briefly?

00:20:58.552 --> 00:21:00.000
- I think that's enough for now

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.140
- Okay.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:05.850
So Caleb and Alice, I think you're gonna

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:07.010
Oh, sorry we're gonna turn over

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:08.460
to Caleb who's gonna speak a little

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:10.310
and then we're gonna talk with Caleb.

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:17.070
- Thanks Rachel and Laura
for that presentation

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:18.590
and for your fantastic book.

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:22.270
Thank you to Alice and
Whitney Humanity Center,

00:21:22.270 --> 00:21:26.030
organizers and staff for inviting me

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:28.860
to be a part of this event.

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:33.860
I have a highest respect for
the rigor and intelligence

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:38.090
and thoughtfulness of this
book, "The Teaching Archive."

00:21:38.090 --> 00:21:41.920
And I'm so excited to
be here to have a chance

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:43.820
to talk about it with you.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:46.707
I just wanted to say a
little bit about the shape of

00:21:46.707 --> 00:21:49.490
the book and what it means to me.

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:51.610
I'll say a little bit
about my own teaching

00:21:51.610 --> 00:21:53.493
I'll mention one of our own.

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:57.910
Some teaching archives
that we have here at Yale.

00:21:57.910 --> 00:22:00.650
And then I'll ask a few questions about

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:03.550
what I take to be some of
the kind of implications

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:05.400
of the book or the bigger arguments

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:09.330
that you've offered to us.

00:22:09.330 --> 00:22:14.190
So this book brings to
teachers of English,

00:22:14.190 --> 00:22:18.070
teachers of literature,
students of English literature,

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:21.700
some very basic questions
and very serious questions.

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:25.820
What is this discipline
of literary studies?

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:28.160
What are its origins?

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:32.420
How has it changed over the
past half century or century?

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:33.903
Who participates in it?

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:37.723
Who shapes its practices,
itself understanding?

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:41.390
What are its characteristics
theories, methods,

00:22:41.390 --> 00:22:43.263
arguments, materials?

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:46.850
As Laura and Rachel have mentioned,

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:49.810
a number of histories of the
discipline have been written

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:54.810
and many of them tend
to follow more or less

00:22:55.750 --> 00:22:58.830
by now familiar kind of arc.

00:22:58.830 --> 00:23:01.410
They are histories of prevailing ideas

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:05.180
or in other words histories
of what English professors

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:08.640
have said about what
they're doing in their work.

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:10.270
And by their work they normally mean

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:12.780
their published scholarship.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:14.040
I think it's probably true that

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:17.830
if you ask a number of English
professors about their work,

00:23:17.830 --> 00:23:19.900
they'll take you to be asking about

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:22.110
their writing or about their scholarship.

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:23.940
Whereas if you ask them about their job,

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:25.470
they might take you to be asking

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:27.420
about their teaching and their service.

00:23:29.170 --> 00:23:32.050
So those histories of the discipline

00:23:32.050 --> 00:23:36.430
perhaps tend to focus on
influential books and articles

00:23:36.430 --> 00:23:38.870
in which literary critics

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:42.680
explicitly reflect on their own practices

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:46.340
and the kind of debates of their time.

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:49.110
And this produces an
account of the history

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:51.580
the discipline that sort
of organizing around

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:54.940
some strong disagreements between camps,

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:58.003
philologists versus aesthetics,

00:23:58.003 --> 00:24:00.540
historicists versus formalists,

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:04.190
multi-culturalists versus
the defenders of a cannon.

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:07.470
Those are some of the familiar
kind of patterns that emerge.

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:09.980
And then there's a kind of story

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:11.510
about a big change that happened

00:24:11.510 --> 00:24:14.910
sometime around or after 1968,

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:16.020
when the discipline moves

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:19.490
from the era of the old new critics,

00:24:19.490 --> 00:24:20.920
to a more diversified

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:23.100
and interdisciplinary enterprise,

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:24.520
that's drawing from traditions

00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:27.510
and critical theory, cultural studies

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:31.673
area studies, ethnic
studies and other fields.

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:34.180
And here's where Laura and Rachel

00:24:34.180 --> 00:24:38.143
in "The Teaching Archive"
make their big intervention,

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:43.310
they ask us to look not at
what literature have said

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:47.940
or how they've explicitly
described their project,

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:49.660
but instead look at

00:24:49.660 --> 00:24:53.200
the materials that they
have produced as teachers,

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:55.650
the true history of English literary study

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:58.060
resides in classrooms they write

00:24:58.060 --> 00:25:00.460
in one of their most
canonical moments maybe

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:01.800
in the introduction,

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:04.350
and maybe even more canonically.

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:08.250
Disciplinary historians of
English have by and large

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:11.023
declined to research in their field.

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:14.830
In other words, we won't
really know the history

00:25:14.830 --> 00:25:17.530
of art discipline until
we've studied the history

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:20.320
of its teaching in the classrooms.

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:23.530
So the classroom as a
space of experimentation,

00:25:23.530 --> 00:25:26.400
as a space of research, and creative work

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:30.010
not only the transmission of
knowledge from the teacher

00:25:30.010 --> 00:25:31.340
to the student.

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:35.020
And here they argue against
the kind of separation of

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:36.730
teaching and research

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:40.120
in how the discipline
understands its own history.

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:43.610
And in how our work as well
is measured and assessed,

00:25:43.610 --> 00:25:46.900
whether by each other
or by the universities,

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:49.590
or perhaps by a public.

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:52.640
I wanted to touch on
three kind of consequences

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:55.760
of this a methodological reorientation

00:25:55.760 --> 00:25:58.210
of the history of art discipline.

00:25:58.210 --> 00:26:01.670
One, what they show us is that
in practice literary studies

00:26:01.670 --> 00:26:03.280
draws from many methods

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:07.460
mixing formal analysis with
historical contextualization,

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:10.820
mixing close reading
with ideology critique,

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:14.350
mixing thematic question and complicated

00:26:15.200 --> 00:26:17.360
sort of computational techniques,

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:18.920
somewhat promiscuously.

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:21.580
And as Rachel and Laura mentioned in their

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:24.440
introductory remarks we would
probably find this to be true

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:26.040
if we were just reading
through the kind of

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:28.870
practical criticism of
any particular period.

00:26:28.870 --> 00:26:30.847
And we certainly find
it in the classrooms.

00:26:30.847 --> 00:26:34.680
And we find it less in kind
of method war situations

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:37.840
where people feel compelled
to align themselves

00:26:37.840 --> 00:26:40.110
with one method or with one argument

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:45.110
or with one concept against
some opponent in a debate.

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:50.940
And so, Laura and Rachel
kinda teaching us how

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:53.410
not to separate those various

00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:55.450
the kind of eclecticism of the discipline

00:26:55.450 --> 00:26:59.870
out into those mutually
antagonistic camps.

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:03.830
Second, there's a kind of
historic graphic argument

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:06.870
about when and how the
era of close reading

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:10.170
or the era of the new
critics gave way to some time

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:13.980
of pluralism in the methods
and objects of literary study.

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:16.160
And then what "The Teaching
Archive" is showing us

00:27:16.160 --> 00:27:19.470
is that it's not really
right to say that there is a

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:23.003
kind of post 1968 diversification,

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:26.980
particularly not a kind
of set of arguments

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:29.400
within elite institutions that then

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:31.930
radiates out and transforms the curricula

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:34.420
at other institutions across the country

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:36.360
or around the world world.

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:39.560
But instead that there were
many different kinds of

00:27:39.560 --> 00:27:42.380
practices going on at many
different kinds of institutions

00:27:42.380 --> 00:27:44.430
all along that, that kind of eclecticism

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:47.890
and diversity of theories
and methods is there.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:50.963
If you go and look for it in
different kinds of classrooms.

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:53.910
Finally, maybe most
controversial literary study

00:27:53.910 --> 00:27:58.210
a set of kind of ethical and
political arguments about

00:27:58.210 --> 00:28:00.253
what happens when we value teaching?

00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:04.160
When we value what
happens in the classroom

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:08.280
and how that might help us
to defend art discipline

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:09.340
in a time of crisis,

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:12.890
how it might help us to
see a kind of common cause

00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:16.260
across a methodological differences,

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:20.350
and the sort of difference that
this new history might make

00:28:20.350 --> 00:28:24.020
in this time of crisis in art discipline.

00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:25.170
Where the question

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:27.810
some people might be asking is less

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:29.730
what is literary studies?

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:32.413
And more of what was literary studies?

00:28:34.040 --> 00:28:37.080
I found this book to be an illumination,

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:39.650
and to resonate with my own experience

00:28:39.650 --> 00:28:43.120
in teaching both in Yale
university classrooms

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:45.340
and in Prison classrooms.

00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:48.190
Two kinds of institutions
that are sort of at the

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:51.570
margins of the big central
picture that Laura and Rachel

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:55.047
are drawing for us in
"The Teaching Archive."

00:28:57.030 --> 00:29:00.230
And in those classrooms, I've found,

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:02.470
especially in teaching
across the two of them,

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:05.430
I've found how teaching with
different groups of students,

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:08.350
even with the same, more
or less the same syllabus

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:12.430
has really caused me
to reorient my sense of

00:29:12.430 --> 00:29:14.960
what counts as remedial in one situation

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:17.510
and what feels advanced
in another situation.

00:29:17.510 --> 00:29:21.200
Or what feels radical in
one situation versus feeling

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:23.860
conservative or even reactionary
in another situation.

00:29:23.860 --> 00:29:27.570
And so, it does seem right to say

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:30.490
that the practical experience of teaching,

00:29:30.490 --> 00:29:32.760
and working with students
in art discipline,

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:36.380
really kind of reorients
and destabilizes some of the

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:40.910
conventional and rigid
oppositions that we make

00:29:40.910 --> 00:29:43.120
when we argue perhaps badly about

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:44.730
what it is that we're doing.

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:46.490
I wanted to show a couple of images.

00:29:46.490 --> 00:29:49.560
These are not from my
own teaching archive,

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:52.040
but they are archival images held here

00:29:52.040 --> 00:29:52.873
at Yale and the Beinecke.

00:29:52.873 --> 00:29:55.860
This is James Weldon Johnson,

00:29:55.860 --> 00:30:00.170
in his classroom at Fisk
University around 1933.

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:03.820
I show it partly because
we're all nostalgic,

00:30:03.820 --> 00:30:06.810
and miss being in
classrooms with each other

00:30:06.810 --> 00:30:08.700
and miss working with each other.

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:12.090
But also because as soon
as you see this image,

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:14.610
you see how it raises
such complicated questions

00:30:14.610 --> 00:30:18.380
about the elitism and
anti-elitism about hierarchy,

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:21.990
and equality about
democratization and integration

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:25.860
in the context of Fisk
University in the 1930s.

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:30.800
My second image is from
Johnson's own teaching notes.

00:30:30.800 --> 00:30:33.620
And this is a list of paper titles

00:30:33.620 --> 00:30:38.500
of the papers that his
students wrote for the course.

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:41.030
I brought it to partly because

00:30:41.030 --> 00:30:45.380
of the little cross out that
you'll see there on number six

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:50.890
which says comparison of something,

00:30:50.890 --> 00:30:54.760
value of Douglas' for
different autobiographies.

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:56.530
This is a student's paper title.

00:30:56.530 --> 00:30:58.640
And what comes before value's

00:30:58.640 --> 00:31:01.860
originally written in as literary
and then it's crossed out

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:04.560
and the word historical
has been inserted there.

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:07.210
So that kind of interplay of the literary

00:31:07.210 --> 00:31:09.910
and historical seems so kind of central

00:31:09.910 --> 00:31:14.130
to Rachel and Laura's
arguments in the books,

00:31:14.130 --> 00:31:16.500
and also raised for me what
would be an interesting question

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:20.050
about how the archival scholar
would deal with a kind of

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:21.220
with an instance like this,

00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:24.290
where the archive presents
us with some ambiguity

00:31:24.290 --> 00:31:25.410
that we have to resolve,

00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:28.810
or some revision that we would
need to make some decisions

00:31:28.810 --> 00:31:30.710
about what it's meaning was.

00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:33.090
But the other reason that I
wanted to share this picture,

00:31:33.090 --> 00:31:37.460
was just to give you the
sense of Johnson's sense

00:31:37.460 --> 00:31:40.060
that an important thing
that his students were

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:41.730
working toward in that classroom,

00:31:41.730 --> 00:31:43.950
was the writing of these papers.

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:45.083
In fact Johnson,

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:49.890
typed up this list of
papers and included it

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:51.350
the next time he taught this course,

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:53.960
as in part of the course description

00:31:53.960 --> 00:31:57.240
to say this is the kind of
work that students would be

00:31:57.240 --> 00:31:59.493
doing in his course.

00:32:00.640 --> 00:32:04.550
So I wanted to close with
a couple of questions.

00:32:04.550 --> 00:32:08.880
And these questions come
out of my own experience

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:09.713
as a teacher,

00:32:09.713 --> 00:32:12.110
or come out of my own
experience as a researcher

00:32:12.110 --> 00:32:14.260
in histories of discipline.

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:17.060
And are partly prompted by
some of what we've just seen

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:19.883
and the archival things
we've looked at together.

00:32:20.930 --> 00:32:25.780
First I guess a question
about discipline itself

00:32:25.780 --> 00:32:28.250
and how we value it.

00:32:28.250 --> 00:32:30.840
One way to describe the project
of "The Teaching Archive."

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:33.270
I think, and Laura and Rachel can maybe

00:32:33.270 --> 00:32:35.070
respond to this characterization is that

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:37.990
they have reoriented our
history of the discipline

00:32:37.990 --> 00:32:40.480
of English or the discipline
of literary studies,

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:44.450
by bringing it within the
historiography of education.

00:32:44.450 --> 00:32:46.280
In other words they're
writing a history of teaching,

00:32:46.280 --> 00:32:48.200
they're writing history of classrooms.

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:49.610
That is its own discipline,

00:32:49.610 --> 00:32:51.950
education and the history of education.

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:55.190
And it has its own interesting
kind of transformations

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:57.570
in the post 1968 period,

00:32:57.570 --> 00:33:00.390
specifically a kind of critique

00:33:00.390 --> 00:33:04.010
of a traditional liberal
account of the value

00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:08.360
or the meaning of education
in public and otherwise.

00:33:08.360 --> 00:33:11.750
There was in the post 1968
period that really kind of

00:33:12.670 --> 00:33:15.510
radical critique of
educational institutions.

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:17.460
This is we can associate this with

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:19.230
full close work on knowledge and power,

00:33:19.230 --> 00:33:22.270
but also major critiques by post-colonial,

00:33:22.270 --> 00:33:24.060
and indigenous scholars,

00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:26.540
who were thinking about the Alliance

00:33:26.540 --> 00:33:30.590
between educational
institutions and politics

00:33:30.590 --> 00:33:35.140
and forms of power that they

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:37.890
very much saw themselves in opposition to.

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:41.230
And so I wonder what that kind of account

00:33:41.230 --> 00:33:46.230
of what a classroom is, might
do with and in conversation

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:49.440
with the kind of story that
Rachel and Laura are telling us

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:50.740
in "The Teaching Archive,"

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:54.810
which is so much a book about

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:57.390
reminding us or teaching
us again how to value,

00:33:57.390 --> 00:33:58.580
what happens in classrooms,

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:01.410
how to value education
and its institutions

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:04.363
across different kinds of
social stratifications.

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:08.210
Along with that, I wanted
to raise a question

00:34:08.210 --> 00:34:10.900
about the teaching of writing,

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:15.843
and the the Josephine Miles
example of English 1A,

00:34:16.840 --> 00:34:18.010
really helps us to get there,

00:34:18.010 --> 00:34:21.100
I think that's a place where
the book is really thinking

00:34:21.100 --> 00:34:22.840
about the composition classroom.

00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:26.390
There's one question I did
have in reading the text,

00:34:26.390 --> 00:34:29.670
had to do with whether the
emphasis on literary studies

00:34:29.670 --> 00:34:30.683
and reading,

00:34:32.278 --> 00:34:35.880
how that fits in relation to
the kind of stratification

00:34:35.880 --> 00:34:37.820
or division between the
teaching of literature,

00:34:37.820 --> 00:34:39.370
and the teaching of writing?

00:34:39.370 --> 00:34:41.740
Both of which might be
thought of as very integral

00:34:41.740 --> 00:34:43.970
to the history of English as a discipline,

00:34:43.970 --> 00:34:46.120
but which are increasingly
kind of separated

00:34:46.120 --> 00:34:46.970
out from each other

00:34:46.970 --> 00:34:51.950
and in hierarchized ways
within universities today.

00:34:51.950 --> 00:34:54.600
In my own research,

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:56.310
and my own writing,

00:34:56.310 --> 00:34:59.030
I've sometimes made an
argument that we need to

00:34:59.030 --> 00:35:02.890
learn to see close reading
itself as a form of writing.

00:35:02.890 --> 00:35:07.047
And I realized that a kind
of critique of that implicit

00:35:09.720 --> 00:35:13.300
in 'The Teaching Archive" might be to say,

00:35:13.300 --> 00:35:15.188
that I am doing the thing

00:35:15.188 --> 00:35:19.380
that Laura and Rachel accused
disciplinary historians

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:21.650
and theorists of doing when they say

00:35:21.650 --> 00:35:25.060
that we overemphasize
our published writing,

00:35:25.060 --> 00:35:28.343
and under-emphasized what
happens in the classroom.

00:35:29.180 --> 00:35:31.880
But as often felt to
me that thinking about

00:35:31.880 --> 00:35:33.280
literary criticism,

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:36.330
about what's called reading
as a practice of writing,

00:35:36.330 --> 00:35:38.350
can have the other effect as well.

00:35:38.350 --> 00:35:41.760
That is, it links up with
the teaching of composition.

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:44.420
It links up with the teaching of writing,

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:47.000
and makes those things part
of a continuous practice

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:50.220
rather than insisting on
a division between them.

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:52.090
So I'd really be interested to hear,

00:35:52.090 --> 00:35:54.550
and maybe I could just bring Laura

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:56.700
and Rachel and Alice all back on now.

00:35:56.700 --> 00:35:59.550
And I present those to
you as open questions

00:35:59.550 --> 00:36:03.357
and the starting points
for our conversation.

00:36:06.180 --> 00:36:07.410
- Thank you Caleb.

00:36:07.410 --> 00:36:09.430
And I'm encouraging people to

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:12.810
enter their intervention in the Q and A,

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:16.440
but meanwhile I'll pass
the floor to our authors

00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:17.690
for some responses,

00:36:17.690 --> 00:36:21.320
and I'll help with responses due to that.

00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:25.070
Great intervention by Caleb.

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:26.010
- Yeah, thanks Caleb.

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:27.960
I mean, we could spend the last 20 minutes

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:29.500
just on one of these questions,

00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:31.800
so maybe we'll try to
touch really briefly,

00:36:31.800 --> 00:36:34.453
on them and make sure
we have time for others.

00:36:35.800 --> 00:36:39.830
But yes, I mean, I think we
also see that full Codian turn

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:40.820
in literary studies too,

00:36:40.820 --> 00:36:41.810
we have the same debate

00:36:41.810 --> 00:36:43.330
about the history of the novel, right?

00:36:43.330 --> 00:36:44.413
And how we're gonna,

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:47.050
I think the thing I'd
emphasize about our work,

00:36:47.050 --> 00:36:50.570
is that we are trying to
value but not glamorize

00:36:50.570 --> 00:36:53.820
or paint portraits of like great teachers,

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:57.630
but really just trying to think, not just,

00:36:57.630 --> 00:36:58.850
but really trying to think more

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:01.690
about what practice looked like.

00:37:01.690 --> 00:37:04.710
And I think that those
full Codian critiques

00:37:04.710 --> 00:37:06.400
of education have still been real

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:09.650
and the disciplinary historic
works that do engage them,

00:37:09.650 --> 00:37:12.600
have been really important to us.

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:15.480
I mean, the same thing that
people point out about Full code

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:17.740
and its uses is true for the (indistinct)

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:20.050
sort of the history
aggregate education turn,

00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:22.980
which is that full code is
not saying you can undo that

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:26.140
those aspects of
institutions.(chuckles) Right?

00:37:26.140 --> 00:37:29.100
And so, it's all we
think part of the history

00:37:29.100 --> 00:37:31.000
of the discipline and that's
what we're trying to get at.

00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:33.910
Laura would probably have
things to add to that?

00:37:33.910 --> 00:37:35.240
- Yeah, I mean, I think,

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:38.170
when we started working on
this about 10 years ago,

00:37:38.170 --> 00:37:41.320
I think there was a sense in
literary studies particularly

00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:43.810
in the wake of Guillory's
Cultural Capital,

00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:48.090
and conversations around that,

00:37:48.090 --> 00:37:50.790
that there was, you know

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:53.550
Guillory is making an
argument essentially about

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:55.270
in the midst of the culture Wars,

00:37:55.270 --> 00:37:57.133
about the contents of reading lists,

00:37:58.069 --> 00:38:01.680
and suggesting that nearly adding,

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:03.540
minority authors onto reading lists

00:38:03.540 --> 00:38:06.026
in order to make them more representative

00:38:06.026 --> 00:38:10.940
kind of ignores the larger
function of the school itself

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:15.433
and in the reproduction of
homogeneity of hierarchies.

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:19.270
And that sort of left,

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:20.980
I think that left people
with a sense almost

00:38:20.980 --> 00:38:23.730
that it didn't really
matter, what they taught,

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:24.780
in their classrooms.

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:25.793
And that this was,

00:38:27.310 --> 00:38:28.680
it was easy for people to feel that way

00:38:28.680 --> 00:38:31.900
because there already wasn't
much public conversation

00:38:31.900 --> 00:38:35.040
beyond the culture Wars,
around common conversation

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:36.600
around classroom teaching.

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:38.810
And I think there was a tendency
to kind of have this all

00:38:38.810 --> 00:38:40.790
or nothing fantasy about classrooms.

00:38:40.790 --> 00:38:45.150
Like either classrooms
were sort of all powerful,

00:38:45.150 --> 00:38:48.580
and reproductive or you could never hope

00:38:48.580 --> 00:38:51.920
to accomplish anything
(laughs) in your classroom

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:53.170
that would extend down to the real world.

00:38:53.170 --> 00:38:54.410
So I think it would, it begin,

00:38:54.410 --> 00:38:56.270
we were really just
looking for something more

00:38:56.270 --> 00:38:58.580
of a middle ground of
what it actually felt like

00:38:58.580 --> 00:39:02.110
that some things failed,
something succeeded,

00:39:02.110 --> 00:39:04.610
most things that were
happening were not entirely

00:39:04.610 --> 00:39:06.260
under your control anyways.

00:39:06.260 --> 00:39:10.133
And to sort of begin to develop
a vocabulary around that.

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:13.870
I think in response to
your second question

00:39:13.870 --> 00:39:18.080
about the connection or
this kind of maybe break

00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:20.030
between the teaching of composition

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:22.360
and the teaching of literature,

00:39:22.360 --> 00:39:23.193
I will, you know,

00:39:23.193 --> 00:39:24.790
you can definitely see the formation

00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:27.840
of that discipline and the
growing split between them,

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:31.690
and the different labor
practices that ensue

00:39:31.690 --> 00:39:33.370
around the teaching of composition

00:39:33.370 --> 00:39:35.080
and the teaching of literature.

00:39:35.080 --> 00:39:37.360
I will say the history of comp,

00:39:37.360 --> 00:39:39.470
they have done a much better job

00:39:39.470 --> 00:39:41.350
of historicizing their teaching.

00:39:41.350 --> 00:39:44.360
They have really excellent
disciplinary histories.

00:39:44.360 --> 00:39:45.800
They have a really good sense

00:39:45.800 --> 00:39:49.873
of the kind of communal
property of their pedagogy.

00:39:50.820 --> 00:39:55.120
And I think, in literary study,

00:39:55.120 --> 00:39:57.430
the Miles' chapter is a
really good example of this.

00:39:57.430 --> 00:40:00.310
There's just really
unrecognized genealogies

00:40:00.310 --> 00:40:03.507
of literary study that emerged
from the teaching of writing

00:40:03.507 --> 00:40:05.310
and the teaching of composition.

00:40:05.310 --> 00:40:08.450
And I think given that
so many of us do both,

00:40:08.450 --> 00:40:10.570
always have done both,

00:40:10.570 --> 00:40:13.760
in terms of just our
graduate student TA ships.

00:40:13.760 --> 00:40:16.823
And then what a lot of people's
jobs look like nowadays,

00:40:17.660 --> 00:40:20.300
you know, we really haven't
well accounted I think

00:40:20.300 --> 00:40:23.860
for that continual kind
of cross-pollinization

00:40:23.860 --> 00:40:25.520
around those kinds of courses.

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:28.173
And then these upper
division English courses.

00:40:29.610 --> 00:40:32.700
- If I could step in,
I was so moved because

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:35.386
of course I took English 1A at Berkeley,

00:40:35.386 --> 00:40:38.091
(indistinct)

00:40:38.091 --> 00:40:41.640
and I have a very vivid
memory of reading Lolita

00:40:41.640 --> 00:40:43.506
in English 1A,

00:40:43.506 --> 00:40:44.700
- Yeah.

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:47.840
- Yeah, and I also thought
it was incredible the way

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:51.640
Miles had them go stand on
a street corner in Berkeley,

00:40:51.640 --> 00:40:54.060
because it's the exact method of

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:57.760
the avant-garde French literary
movement, "the Oulipo."

00:40:57.760 --> 00:41:00.350
I mean, Josh paddock who set himself up

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:01.760
on the plus sensory piece

00:41:01.760 --> 00:41:06.760
to observe the totally Beno,
you know, for hours and hours.

00:41:07.100 --> 00:41:09.980
And she sounds like an artist.

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:11.820
I mean, she was hat.

00:41:11.820 --> 00:41:13.800
It's almost like pre situationism

00:41:13.800 --> 00:41:16.460
like she was having them do.

00:41:16.460 --> 00:41:18.370
And I don't know if she was aware of that,

00:41:18.370 --> 00:41:21.420
but it's a fantastic exercise

00:41:21.420 --> 00:41:24.383
in documentary writing that she was doing.

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:26.580
That was one comment.

00:41:26.580 --> 00:41:29.470
And my other comment is about teaching

00:41:29.470 --> 00:41:32.840
composition versus teaching literature.

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:34.330
The equivalent of that in the

00:41:34.330 --> 00:41:36.070
National Literature's of course,

00:41:36.070 --> 00:41:38.640
is teaching beginning language

00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:41.973
versus teaching the great works.

00:41:42.870 --> 00:41:46.180
And so I think those of us
in the National Literature's,

00:41:46.180 --> 00:41:51.180
we all started as teachers
teaching elementary grammar.

00:41:51.410 --> 00:41:54.420
Which is even more of a distinction

00:41:54.420 --> 00:41:58.160
than teaching writing and
teaching English literature.

00:41:58.160 --> 00:42:01.610
And I've always thought that
we have very different styles

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:03.870
because of that as teachers.

00:42:03.870 --> 00:42:08.870
Because we start out learning
that what you do is listen.

00:42:10.340 --> 00:42:13.430
So it does really change
the authority dynamic

00:42:13.430 --> 00:42:14.263
in the classroom.

00:42:14.263 --> 00:42:16.220
You just, 'case you have.

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:18.500
These are more like comments
than questions for you

00:42:18.500 --> 00:42:23.500
but I wonder if it evokes
something for all of you.

00:42:23.810 --> 00:42:25.140
- Yeah that's illuminating.

00:42:25.140 --> 00:42:27.960
Cause it hadn't occurred to me

00:42:27.960 --> 00:42:29.960
but it's also true that
it's kind of almost

00:42:29.960 --> 00:42:31.480
closer to what Miles is doing.

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:35.551
The idea of teaching
at the level of grammar

00:42:35.551 --> 00:42:36.500
(chuckles)

00:42:36.500 --> 00:42:39.530
and language than assuming
that our students already know

00:42:39.530 --> 00:42:41.270
the structure of the sentence,
the structure of a paragraph.

00:42:41.270 --> 00:42:43.253
It is more like language learning.

00:42:44.180 --> 00:42:47.210
- Yeah, Caleb.

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:50.300
- Yeah that's also true.

00:42:50.300 --> 00:42:52.600
You would find that in
the James Weldon Johnson

00:42:52.600 --> 00:42:55.340
teaching archive as well
that his introductory course

00:42:55.340 --> 00:42:57.240
was a language and literature course,

00:42:57.240 --> 00:42:59.783
although it's in the English department,

00:43:00.690 --> 00:43:05.630
but has a good deal of grammar
and vocabulary built into

00:43:06.520 --> 00:43:08.683
the literary reading that they're doing.

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:12.560
- We've got a couple questions.

00:43:12.560 --> 00:43:16.840
Here's one, somebody who
values immensely the turn

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:19.220
of the everyday practice
of the discipline rather

00:43:19.220 --> 00:43:22.700
than its manifesto statements
or public quarrels,

00:43:22.700 --> 00:43:27.650
but wonders how you make the
bridge from that oral place

00:43:27.650 --> 00:43:30.930
in context, specific
location of the classroom

00:43:30.930 --> 00:43:33.960
to the public published
and circulated domain

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:37.440
of written criticism,
which has its own overlook

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:39.683
everyday practices of written style.

00:43:43.740 --> 00:43:45.210
- Yeah, that's a good point.

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:47.750
I mean, there's really interesting
recent work by Jonathan.

00:43:47.750 --> 00:43:50.400
Kramnick on exactly this topic,

00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:54.420
on how the every day the
kind of real practical,

00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:58.860
continuous work of published
criticism and literary studies

00:43:58.860 --> 00:44:01.510
focuses on, like anybody
goes inline quotation,

00:44:01.510 --> 00:44:05.120
just studying the
incorporation of the texts

00:44:05.120 --> 00:44:07.420
that you're looking at
into your own language.

00:44:08.810 --> 00:44:09.720
- I think, yeah.

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:11.050
I mean, we try to make them,

00:44:11.050 --> 00:44:13.610
when we say that we're
able to see classrooms

00:44:13.610 --> 00:44:16.730
kind of present within works of criticism

00:44:16.730 --> 00:44:18.363
with a major works of criticism.

00:44:19.310 --> 00:44:22.580
We said that in that
kind of brief form here

00:44:22.580 --> 00:44:26.000
but we can expand on that
a little bit I think.

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:28.110
Even with the example of the Eliot

00:44:29.670 --> 00:44:32.633
and you know I think one of
the things we notice is that,

00:44:32.633 --> 00:44:34.670
there's sort of ethos of the classroom

00:44:34.670 --> 00:44:36.440
that play specific role and very

00:44:36.440 --> 00:44:41.440
communal scene of valuing
literature does get worked up

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:44.800
in places like "The Sacred Wood"

00:44:44.800 --> 00:44:49.500
or in even in Miles' work
on poetry rather than poems

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:52.370
into a kind of vision
of literary tradition

00:44:52.370 --> 00:44:53.750
that is very populous

00:44:53.750 --> 00:44:56.227
that is populated by what Eliot calls

00:44:56.227 --> 00:44:58.260
"second order minds,"

00:44:58.260 --> 00:45:01.420
critics, translators,
writers of para text.

00:45:01.420 --> 00:45:06.320
And so, there is I think a
sense that when you pivot to

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:09.900
the writing of criticism,
you're sort of doing it

00:45:09.900 --> 00:45:11.370
stepping out of the classroom

00:45:11.370 --> 00:45:15.090
with more of a sense of
how many hands are involved

00:45:15.090 --> 00:45:19.230
in the creation of a
piece of published writing

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:22.270
and in the eventual reception of it,

00:45:22.270 --> 00:45:25.210
and hopefully the longer trajectories

00:45:25.210 --> 00:45:26.540
that it makes possible, right?

00:45:26.540 --> 00:45:28.760
By making other kinds of writing possible.

00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:31.480
So that was something
in stepping in for us

00:45:31.480 --> 00:45:33.270
and moving from this classroom scene

00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:34.670
to people's published scholarship,

00:45:34.670 --> 00:45:37.756
we started to pay
attention to these ways of

00:45:37.756 --> 00:45:39.950
thinking about literary
tradition, literary value,

00:45:39.950 --> 00:45:41.900
and the practice of writing that seemed

00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:46.020
more populous and communal
than we'd been (indistinct)

00:45:46.020 --> 00:45:50.130
- You know, completely
deconstruct all our mythology

00:45:50.130 --> 00:45:52.300
about the new criticism.

00:45:52.300 --> 00:45:55.110
You completely redo our
sense of the new criticism.

00:45:55.110 --> 00:45:56.730
I'm not like,

00:45:56.730 --> 00:46:01.210
I mean, my generation came
up with the sacred mission

00:46:01.210 --> 00:46:02.970
of fighting the new criticism

00:46:02.970 --> 00:46:06.860
and all it's elitist as in
close reading only assumption.

00:46:06.860 --> 00:46:08.300
So reading about Eliot,

00:46:08.300 --> 00:46:11.050
and it's just, it's quite astonishing.

00:46:11.050 --> 00:46:12.440
Yeah.
- I think that is, I mean,

00:46:12.440 --> 00:46:16.020
I think in critiquing the new
criticism and another things

00:46:16.020 --> 00:46:19.610
there has been a tendency
to make it more unified,

00:46:19.610 --> 00:46:23.220
to make it bigger than it in
fact was, or more coherent

00:46:23.220 --> 00:46:25.680
or more dominant even.

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:28.610
So I feel like too, this book
kind of comes after a wave

00:46:28.610 --> 00:46:32.420
of critique that sort of
ended up making many things

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:34.020
a much bigger deal

00:46:34.020 --> 00:46:37.190
than they perhaps were
in the first instance

00:46:38.240 --> 00:46:39.600
- Another Question?

00:46:39.600 --> 00:46:41.510
Oh, sorry Caleb did you
want to say something

00:46:41.510 --> 00:46:43.291
before I move to the next question?

00:46:43.291 --> 00:46:44.400
- Yeah, I mean, I just,

00:46:44.400 --> 00:46:46.636
I wanted to pick up on Laura's phrase

00:46:46.636 --> 00:46:48.773
like what kind of writing is possible,

00:46:50.370 --> 00:46:54.130
cause that sort of how I
think about a method Wars

00:46:54.130 --> 00:46:55.273
in a way like,

00:46:56.110 --> 00:46:58.513
it's so obviously wrong that,

00:46:59.620 --> 00:47:02.640
you know, that the political
economy of our profession

00:47:02.640 --> 00:47:06.335
is in decline because of
too much ideology critique,

00:47:06.335 --> 00:47:07.210
(laughs)

00:47:07.210 --> 00:47:11.220
for the political economy of
our profession is in decline

00:47:11.220 --> 00:47:15.000
because we don't love literature
or something like that.

00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:18.130
So obviously wrong, but it makes
me want to ask the question

00:47:18.130 --> 00:47:21.063
like what's being enabled by this mistake.

00:47:22.730 --> 00:47:27.730
what are people getting from
getting it wrong in this way?

00:47:28.100 --> 00:47:32.280
And I think it, that they
get to treat a problem

00:47:32.280 --> 00:47:33.950
with political economy,

00:47:33.950 --> 00:47:35.360
as if it were the kind of problem

00:47:35.360 --> 00:47:37.180
that they know how to
address in their writing.

00:47:37.180 --> 00:47:38.630
As if it were an intellectual problem,

00:47:38.630 --> 00:47:39.720
as if it were a misreading,

00:47:39.720 --> 00:47:41.523
as if it were a conceptual mistake.

00:47:43.220 --> 00:47:47.080
And that could be addressed
therefore argumentation,

00:47:47.080 --> 00:47:49.010
through the kinds of skills
that they know how to have,

00:47:49.010 --> 00:47:50.290
and through the kinds of disagreements

00:47:50.290 --> 00:47:52.320
that we know how to have
with each other in public,

00:47:52.320 --> 00:47:55.560
rather than through a
work of a different kind,

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:57.427
which would have to do with organizing

00:47:57.427 --> 00:48:00.053
and the transformation of
institutional structures

00:48:00.053 --> 00:48:02.093
and economic circumstances.

00:48:03.610 --> 00:48:06.650
And in that way, I mean, I do think that,

00:48:07.700 --> 00:48:11.330
offering a new history is
a little more continuous

00:48:11.330 --> 00:48:13.530
than totally distinct
from that particular move.

00:48:13.530 --> 00:48:17.111
That if we see this different history,

00:48:17.111 --> 00:48:19.940
I think we can do is
write a history, right?

00:48:19.940 --> 00:48:21.970
I think we can do as Scott
specifically in our role

00:48:23.384 --> 00:48:25.760
as a scholar is write a
history and change the history.

00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:28.070
And maybe that history
can, I hope it will,

00:48:28.070 --> 00:48:29.780
I think it is,

00:48:29.780 --> 00:48:32.270
changing our understanding
of the value of what we do,

00:48:32.270 --> 00:48:34.630
but it's another step

00:48:34.630 --> 00:48:37.523
to transforming that
political economy obviously.

00:48:38.450 --> 00:48:40.640
- Yeah, we talked about this a
little bit in our conclusion,

00:48:40.640 --> 00:48:43.430
but we hope I think part
of what this history does

00:48:43.430 --> 00:48:48.430
is make the work of teaching
more clearly, right?

00:48:48.670 --> 00:48:51.580
Like part of all the work that we're doing

00:48:51.580 --> 00:48:52.960
in higher education

00:48:52.960 --> 00:48:55.450
and not separated from like our work

00:48:55.450 --> 00:48:57.530
which is our research that
we also like to think of

00:48:57.530 --> 00:48:59.420
and which is labor.

00:48:59.420 --> 00:49:01.420
And so we kind of,

00:49:01.420 --> 00:49:05.680
I completely agree with your
diagnosis of method wars.

00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:07.360
It's like we're trying to domesticate them

00:49:07.360 --> 00:49:10.160
into something we can solve,
within the English department.

00:49:10.160 --> 00:49:10.993
And that's just clearly,

00:49:10.993 --> 00:49:11.826
(chuckles)

00:49:11.826 --> 00:49:13.000
that's a tragic, tragic mistake

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:17.770
but we do hope in some way that
there's work that you can do

00:49:17.770 --> 00:49:21.970
in the discipline to think
about labor in a nuanced

00:49:21.970 --> 00:49:25.870
and granular way that
might help the problem.

00:49:25.870 --> 00:49:29.473
- It's blow to alienation
and division school.

00:49:30.370 --> 00:49:31.840
Let me go on with questions.

00:49:31.840 --> 00:49:33.400
We've now suddenly got a lot of

00:49:33.400 --> 00:49:34.780
really interesting questions.

00:49:34.780 --> 00:49:38.930
This one is about teaching
online during the pandemic

00:49:38.930 --> 00:49:40.660
but also in general,

00:49:40.660 --> 00:49:44.390
on what your work tells
you about the growth

00:49:44.390 --> 00:49:48.440
of online teaching generally
versus face-to-face classroom,

00:49:48.440 --> 00:49:50.840
especially the growth of online teaching

00:49:50.840 --> 00:49:52.360
in community colleges.

00:49:52.360 --> 00:49:53.890
And what do you see happening

00:49:53.890 --> 00:49:57.433
in the classrooms and the courses?

00:50:01.814 --> 00:50:04.410
(laughs)

00:50:04.410 --> 00:50:06.930
- No, No, It's, I mean, yeah,

00:50:06.930 --> 00:50:10.133
I think, you know, we,

00:50:11.370 --> 00:50:12.900
I think many of universities were already

00:50:12.900 --> 00:50:14.940
beginning to go online
before the pandemic,

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:18.140
particularly in the asynchronous mode.

00:50:18.140 --> 00:50:20.540
I did training to do
that at my university.

00:50:20.540 --> 00:50:21.610
There was a lot of incentive,

00:50:21.610 --> 00:50:24.653
financial incentive for professors
to move teaching online.

00:50:25.767 --> 00:50:30.577
And one thing I noticed
in the training is that

00:50:31.729 --> 00:50:32.830
you're very you know,

00:50:32.830 --> 00:50:35.580
they're telling you essentially

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:39.420
to leave out anything
remotely resembling kind of

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:40.269
scholarly expertise.

00:50:40.269 --> 00:50:41.102
(chuckles)

00:50:41.102 --> 00:50:42.200
Like it's like sort of,

00:50:42.200 --> 00:50:43.973
you have to really like kind of,

00:50:45.660 --> 00:50:48.720
give people the three
minute recorded video

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:50.540
no longer than this and so on.

00:50:50.540 --> 00:50:54.010
And it hasn't been my experience
that that's necessarily

00:50:54.010 --> 00:50:59.010
what students want or
are capable of receiving.

00:50:59.070 --> 00:51:01.043
So I do think that there's,
we talk a fair bit.

00:51:01.043 --> 00:51:03.993
And then just the conclusion
to the book about,

00:51:05.960 --> 00:51:09.000
some of the ways in which in
the modern research university

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:11.350
there is a whole industry
built up now around

00:51:11.350 --> 00:51:13.450
telling professors how to teach, right?

00:51:13.450 --> 00:51:15.563
And giving them advice about teaching.

00:51:17.230 --> 00:51:20.010
- Yeah, Centers for teaching
and learning all over,

00:51:20.010 --> 00:51:23.030
but also visits somebody.

00:51:23.030 --> 00:51:27.290
Another interesting
question on the chat is

00:51:27.290 --> 00:51:30.860
what about evaluation of
teaching by colleagues

00:51:30.860 --> 00:51:34.863
in the course of a tenure
review or a promotion review?

00:51:36.960 --> 00:51:38.413
How does that work?

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:47.273
- Yeah.

00:51:48.522 --> 00:51:51.200
- I mean, are we capable of

00:51:51.200 --> 00:51:53.860
I'm just imagining an
extension of this question.

00:51:53.860 --> 00:51:57.440
Are we capable of evaluating
someone's teaching

00:51:57.440 --> 00:52:01.090
without understanding this larger history,

00:52:01.090 --> 00:52:04.250
and seeing one person's
classroom in the context

00:52:04.250 --> 00:52:08.983
of all these decisions that
teachers have made over time?

00:52:10.130 --> 00:52:11.580
- Right, I like the idea

00:52:11.580 --> 00:52:15.860
that this can help having a
deeper sense of the history,

00:52:15.860 --> 00:52:19.540
could help a more professional
and contextualized,

00:52:19.540 --> 00:52:22.990
disciplinarily contextualized
evaluation of teaching.

00:52:22.990 --> 00:52:24.850
It's interesting because so

00:52:24.850 --> 00:52:26.310
when people do look at classrooms

00:52:26.310 --> 00:52:27.730
especially the earlier classrooms,

00:52:27.730 --> 00:52:29.530
that we've looked at
in scholarly research,

00:52:29.530 --> 00:52:32.290
they tend to only look at
the teaching evaluation.

00:52:32.290 --> 00:52:35.460
So there is this whole
kind of interesting history

00:52:35.460 --> 00:52:37.980
of teaching evaluations
being used to stand in

00:52:37.980 --> 00:52:38.854
for classrooms.

00:52:38.854 --> 00:52:40.270
The way I think they very much are

00:52:40.270 --> 00:52:44.420
in tenure decisions in which
anyone cares at all about them.

00:52:44.420 --> 00:52:47.250
- It's funny, there's this
rumor going on in Europe

00:52:47.250 --> 00:52:51.230
that Americans are given
grades by their students

00:52:51.230 --> 00:52:54.539
and they're really appalled by it.

00:52:54.539 --> 00:52:56.622
(laughs)

00:52:59.028 --> 00:53:01.195
- It's sort of true, yeah.

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:03.513
- I've got one.

00:53:03.513 --> 00:53:05.913
I think we have time
for one more question.

00:53:07.350 --> 00:53:10.770
Did you sense that there's some connection

00:53:10.770 --> 00:53:15.420
between teaching strategies
and research methods

00:53:15.420 --> 00:53:18.070
but also literary production?

00:53:18.070 --> 00:53:22.230
I mean, do some people's
way of teaching actually

00:53:22.230 --> 00:53:23.300
feed right in it?

00:53:23.300 --> 00:53:25.030
Sounds like it's true of Eliot,

00:53:25.030 --> 00:53:28.950
but other examples of the
way that teaching inspires

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:30.220
literary production.

00:53:30.220 --> 00:53:32.880
Now I take it that means not
just critical production,

00:53:32.880 --> 00:53:34.880
but the production of literary works.

00:53:34.880 --> 00:53:36.500
Maybe fiction or,

00:53:36.500 --> 00:53:38.990
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:53:38.990 --> 00:53:41.130
I think probably the best
example of this in the book

00:53:41.130 --> 00:53:43.220
is J. Saunders Redding,

00:53:43.220 --> 00:53:47.170
who is teaching you know,

00:53:47.170 --> 00:53:50.470
he's teaching at Hampton
and the '30s, '40s and '50s,

00:53:50.470 --> 00:53:52.680
courses like Negro and American Literature

00:53:52.680 --> 00:53:54.603
but also courses on the novel.

00:53:56.020 --> 00:53:57.633
And he's also writing,

00:53:58.570 --> 00:54:03.160
in a couple of different
genres that are realist.

00:54:03.160 --> 00:54:04.730
So he's writing a documentary,

00:54:04.730 --> 00:54:07.130
he's writing a campus novel,

00:54:07.130 --> 00:54:11.570
and he sort of writing all
around this kind of question

00:54:11.570 --> 00:54:14.310
of realist representation,

00:54:14.310 --> 00:54:15.980
as he is teaching in the classroom.

00:54:15.980 --> 00:54:17.170
So he's teaching Dickens,

00:54:17.170 --> 00:54:19.100
thinking about how Dickens's autobiography

00:54:19.100 --> 00:54:22.390
gets written into some of his fiction,

00:54:22.390 --> 00:54:24.990
at the same time that he's
kind of writing all around

00:54:24.990 --> 00:54:26.170
that matrix as well.

00:54:26.170 --> 00:54:28.310
So that was a good example
of where we really needed

00:54:28.310 --> 00:54:30.840
to look at the fiction and
the literary production

00:54:30.840 --> 00:54:32.490
in order to understand what
he was doing in the classroom.

00:54:32.490 --> 00:54:33.323
- Yeah.

00:54:33.323 --> 00:54:35.970
It's fascinating that you
mentioned the campus novel,

00:54:35.970 --> 00:54:38.570
because it is a genre that's just exploded

00:54:38.570 --> 00:54:40.470
over the last 30 years.

00:54:40.470 --> 00:54:42.630
So there is something fascinating

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:46.720
about the scene of teaching
that appeals to novelists.

00:54:46.720 --> 00:54:49.520
And all those campus
novels are not realist.

00:54:49.520 --> 00:54:52.181
I mean, some of them are just wild.

00:54:52.181 --> 00:54:54.360
(laughs)

00:54:54.360 --> 00:54:56.400
- I guess the obvious,
I mean the obvious thing

00:54:56.400 --> 00:55:00.060
there is a lecture courses
that get basically kind of

00:55:00.060 --> 00:55:03.250
collated to become books often

00:55:03.250 --> 00:55:05.690
kind of introductory
books in various fields.

00:55:05.690 --> 00:55:09.820
But the question about
online teaching definitely

00:55:09.820 --> 00:55:13.720
reminded me that last semester I recorded

00:55:13.720 --> 00:55:15.750
my lectures as audio lectures

00:55:15.750 --> 00:55:18.370
and never as teaching felt
more to me like writing

00:55:18.370 --> 00:55:20.530
than it did then.

00:55:20.530 --> 00:55:23.260
Because it was gonna be recorded.

00:55:23.260 --> 00:55:25.760
I found I can't do this,

00:55:25.760 --> 00:55:28.010
I can't sort of stand in
front of the room and vamp,

00:55:28.010 --> 00:55:29.670
and look at a picture together,

00:55:29.670 --> 00:55:31.670
and interact with the students.

00:55:31.670 --> 00:55:34.480
And because they could
play it back and so on.

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:37.250
I felt almost a level of
like writing for publication.

00:55:37.250 --> 00:55:40.154
Like each word has to
be precise in its way.

00:55:40.154 --> 00:55:43.900
And I think there's
probably a ton of examples

00:55:43.900 --> 00:55:47.290
of places where we could
find some student question,

00:55:47.290 --> 00:55:49.740
or student response to the lecture,

00:55:49.740 --> 00:55:52.090
or even the shape of a lecture
course that ended up changing

00:55:52.090 --> 00:55:53.680
that person's book that was based

00:55:53.680 --> 00:55:56.830
on the lecture course that they gave.

00:55:57.980 --> 00:56:00.110
- Somebody once told
me that when you teach

00:56:00.110 --> 00:56:01.660
you just never know

00:56:01.660 --> 00:56:04.110
what of all the stuff you say

00:56:04.110 --> 00:56:06.480
is gonna stay in the
minds of your students.

00:56:06.480 --> 00:56:09.360
Like some, don't you
remember some just weird

00:56:09.360 --> 00:56:12.870
anecdote that some teacher
told about their dog,

00:56:12.870 --> 00:56:15.140
and you've forgotten the
whole rest of the chorus

00:56:15.140 --> 00:56:18.410
but you remember one anecdote.
(laughs)

00:56:18.410 --> 00:56:19.705
- Absolutely.

00:56:19.705 --> 00:56:21.810
(laughs)

00:56:21.810 --> 00:56:23.620
- All right (indistinct)

00:56:23.620 --> 00:56:27.960
I'm ending on a little
light note here but I just,

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:31.270
I want to thank you all so, so much

00:56:31.270 --> 00:56:32.560
for this conversation,

00:56:32.560 --> 00:56:34.340
that was really, really fun,

00:56:34.340 --> 00:56:39.340
and inspiring and thank
you to all the participants

00:56:39.390 --> 00:56:40.800
for their questions.

00:56:40.800 --> 00:56:44.160
We'll go look on the chat
too, to see your comments

00:56:44.160 --> 00:56:47.350
now that we're done with the Q and A.

00:56:47.350 --> 00:56:48.747
So thank you all
- Go get this book you all

00:56:48.747 --> 00:56:49.880
and read it.

00:56:49.880 --> 00:56:50.713
(chuckles)

00:56:50.713 --> 00:56:53.510
- And please, we are gonna put the link

00:56:53.510 --> 00:56:57.700
with university of Chicago
Press discount code.

00:56:57.700 --> 00:56:59.150
We're putting it in the chat.

00:57:00.030 --> 00:57:01.330
- Thank you so much, Alice.

00:57:01.330 --> 00:57:03.180
And thank you, Caleb.
- Thank you.

00:57:03.180 --> 00:57:04.430
